nodecode-discord-presence is a rewrite of DarRahman's DarRahman/hermes-discord-presence:

  https://github.com/DarRahman/hermes-discord-presence
  commit fb49ff562015b4ae3ed88b2306f2c1de518bfb8b
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Badar Rahman (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the Discord Rich Presence card and everything that keeps it
current — the local IPC conversation with the Discord desktop client (the
handshake, SET_ACTIVITY and the null activity that clears it, the 32-bit
little-endian framing, the discord-ipc-0..9 socket sweep under the runtime
directories, the reconnect every tick and the clear on the way out), the card's
lines (the session line with its generic, full-workspace and hidden modes, the
activity line with the plugin's tool-name vocabulary, the model name and the
turn's token total, the elapsed-time timestamp, the git branch read from
.git/HEAD, the 128-character clipping), and the privacy switches the plugin
exposed (stealth mode, hide model, hide tokens, hide the tool status, hide the
branch). The plugin's settings were kept as the cell's section. The Python
(pypresence, sqlite3, psutil, a background thread) is Common Lisp here: the
socket is SBCL's own sb-bsd-sockets, the JSON is the image's, the session's
numbers come from the turn the agent is running rather than from the harness's
SQLite state database, and one nlk:worker is the tick.

Left out: the plugin's multi-terminal presence ownership (a claim file in the
runtime directory, and herdr's pane-focus socket, which let several Hermes
terminals agree on one Discord card) — a Nodecode cell runs inside one
organism and publishes one card, so the contest does not arise; the "View
Repository" button that parsed the workspace's .git/config for a public
github.com/gitlab.com remote; and the plugin's own Discord application id and
artwork, since the card's name and art are now the operator's own application.
Nothing of the plugin's Python is bundled: this folder is Common Lisp and the
image it runs in.
